          dealing: 'Each player receives 9 cards (the entire deck).',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Score at least 61 points (out of 120 card points) to win the round.',
          gameplay: 'Four players in two partnerships bid 5 to 9 for the right to choose trump. The 6 of the chosen trump suit is highest; then all Kings, Queens, and Jacks in suit order (clubs, spades, hearts, diamonds), then trump suit A-10-9-8-7. Left of dealer leads; must follow suit and must beat the current trick if able. First team to 16 strokes wins.',
          keyRules: [
            'Follow suit; picture cards (J, Q, K) count as trumps.',
            'Must trump if void in led suit; must beat highest card in trick if able.',
            '61+ card points wins the round; 16 strokes win the game.',
            'Tie at 60: redeal same trumps, no bidding. All pass: diamonds, winners score 1 stroke.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Protect the Kings. In Saskop, Kings are the top tier of the trump suit after the 6. If you have the King of Spades, you have the second most powerful card in the game. Use your high trumps to 'Draw' out the opponents' 10s and Aces early. Don't waste your 6 on a trick that doesn't contain at least 10 points.",
          intermediate: "The 'Diamond Doubler': If you have a strong hand in Diamonds, bid aggressively. In most Estonian variants, declaring Diamonds as trumps doubles the value of the game points. This is the fastest way to reach the 16-point goal, but also the fastest way to lose points if the opponents capture your 10s.",
          advanced: "Void Manipulation: Since only 36 cards are in play, suits are short. If you are void in a suit, wait for the opponent to lead it, then 'Slash' it with a medium-rank Jack or Queen. This captures their points without spending your power-cards like the 6 or the Kings. Communication with your partner through 'Throw-away' points is key.",
          tips: [
            "Memorize the 120 total points. If you know you have 55 and your partner just won a trick with a 10, 'Call Out' or stop being defensive.",
            "The 6 is worth zero points but is the ultimate trick-winnerâ€”treat it like a 'Vacuum' for your partner's Aces.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
